{"craft":"craft-of-explanation","evidence":"synthesis-of-scholarship","stance":"counterintuition"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"New scientific research has transformed older theories of personality and offers provocative answers about human nature.","confidence":0.9},"academicOrientation":{"score":30,"confidence":0.92},"profileConfidence":0.9,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"345a1f7e51fead88a5ea2e8fdc6179fcdc829bfc2091fedd40d7139540878a52","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Science","secondarySubjects":["Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores advancements in personality psychology and their implications for understanding human nature, making Science the dominant subject.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"592a4911beb75959fddbbb7bc885f6547933233e35a6333a716e26180df8c74b","summary":"\"In the past few decades, personality psychology has made considerable progress in raising new questions about human nature-and providing some provocative answers. New scientific research has transformed old ideas about personality based on the theories of Freud, Jung, and the humanistic psychologies of the nineteen sixties, which gave rise to the simplistic categorizations of the Meyer-Briggs Inventory and the 'enneagream'. But the general public still knows little about the new science and what it reveals about who we are. In Me, Myself, and Us, Brian Little, one of the psychologists who helped re-shape the field, provides the first in-depth exploration of the new personality science and its provocative findings for general readers. The book explores questions that are rooted in the origins of human consciousness but are as commonplace as yesterday's breakfast conversation. Are our first impressions of other people's personalities usually fallacious? Are creative individuals essentially maladjusted? Are our personality traits, as William James put it \"set like plaster\" by the age of thirty? Is a belief that we are in control of our lives an unmitigated good? Do our singular personalities comprise one unified self or a confederacy of selves, and if the latter, which of our mini-me-s do we offer up in marriage or mergers?","publicationYear":2014,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Well-being","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-me-myself-and-us-brian-r-little"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Personality","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-me-myself-and-us-brian-r-little"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Happiness","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-me-myself-and-us-brian-r-little"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"PSYCHOLOGY / Personality","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-me-myself-and-us-brian-r-little"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"PSYCHOLOGY / Creative Ability","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-me-myself-and-us-brian-r-little"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"PSYCHOLOGY / Developmental / Lifespan Development","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-me-myself-and-us-brian-r-little"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"New York Times reviewed","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-me-myself-and-us-brian-r-little"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"PSYCHOLOGY","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-me-myself-and-us-brian-r-little"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Creative Ability","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-me-myself-and-us-brian-r-little"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Developmental","sourceId":